{:edb/db-before-t 1551 :edb/db-after-t 1552 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534864 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:28:00.510Z" 13194139534864 true] [17592186180825 1104 #edb/ref 17592186180830 13194139534864 true] [17592186183497 1104 #edb/ref 17592186183500 13194139534864 true] [17592186183996 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n\n$\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}$.\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n$\\sin\\left(u + v\\right) = \\dfrac{a- b\\sqrt 7}{20}$,\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b$." 13194139534864 false] [17592186183996 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u =  -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(u + v\\right) = \\dfrac{a- b\\sqrt 7}{20},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139534864 true] [17592186183996 1145 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\cos{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\sin{v}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} v &= 1 - \\cos^{2} v \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{4}{5})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{4^{2}}{5^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{25} - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25 - 16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin v &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (u + v) &= \\sin u\\cos v + \\cos u\\sin v \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5}) + (-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{20} - \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 - 3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3$, and\n\n$$\na+b = 15\n$$" 13194139534864 false] [17592186183996 1145 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u},$ $\\cos{u},$ and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{v},$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5})+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{20}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3,$ and $a+b = 15.$" 13194139534864 true] [17592186184076 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}$.\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v)$." 13194139534864 false] [17592186184076 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v).$" 13194139534864 true] [17592186184076 1145 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} u &= 1 - \\cos^{2} u \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{7}{25})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{7^{2}}{25^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{49}{625} \\\\\n&= \\frac{625}{625} - \\frac{49}{625} \\\\\n&= \\frac{576}{625}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin u &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{24}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sin(u - v) &= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\\\[5pt] &= \\left(-\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {72} {125} - \\dfrac {28} {125} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {100} {125} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {4} {5}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534864 false] [17592186184076 1145 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{7}{25})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right)\n \t\t\t\t -  \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left( \\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {72} {125} -  \\dfrac {28} {125} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {100} {125}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {4} {5}.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139534864 true] [17592186184086 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315785 13194139534864 true] [17592186184086 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315792 13194139534864 true] [17592186184086 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315799 13194139534864 true] [17592186184086 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315813 13194139534864 true] [17592186184086 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315821 13194139534864 true] [17592186184167 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315806 13194139534864 true] [17592186184167 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315817 13194139534864 true] [17592186184167 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315825 13194139534864 true] [17592186184396 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534864 true] [17592186184396 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}, \\cos u = \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}, \\cos v =-\\frac{5}{6}$.\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n$\\sin (u - v) = \\frac{a\\sqrt{5} - b}{24}$,\n\ncalculate the value of $a + b$." 13194139534864 false] [17592186184396 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{5}-b}{24},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139534864 true] [17592186184396 1145 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})(-\\frac{5}{6})-(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{24}-\\frac{11}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-11}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=11,$ and $a+b=16.$" 13194139534864 true] [17592186184768 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534864 true] [17592186184768 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos x =-\\frac{4}{5}, \\sin y = \\frac{12}{13}, \\cos y = \\frac{5}{13}$.\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\sin (y - x)$." 13194139534864 false] [17592186184768 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=\\frac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x).$" 13194139534864 true] [17592186184768 1145 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡x & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}-\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534864 true] [17592186315785 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315786 13194139534864 true] [17592186315785 1089 "q-119451" 13194139534864 true] [17592186315785 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534864 true] [17592186315785 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a-b\\sqrt{55}}{42},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139534864 true] [17592186315785 1113 #uuid "86f6d86f-10c5-5e67-9510-1ded8a2565c7" 13194139534864 true] [17592186315785 1145 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{11})^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36-11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})+(\\frac{5}{6})(\\frac{2}{7